# `nadia.fsx` — the reads that must not raise

Spec: [`docs/specs/total-fs.md`](../docs/specs/total-fs.md).

`std.fs`'s failure behaviour is **undocumented and backend-dependent**: `specs/std-fs-os.md`
maps `listDir` to `Files.list` on the JVM, `fs.readdirSync` on Node and `fs::read_dir` in
Rust, and says nothing about a missing path. The first two raise; the third returns a
`Result`. So the contract a caller programs against is "whatever the host does", which is
not a contract, and every call site has to remember to guard.

That convention held twelve times out of thirteen here. The miss was not random: it was
`misplacedProject`, which runs **only after an acceptance check has already failed** — and
one way a check fails is that the workspace is gone (rozum BUG-017, a sandbox that let an
agent delete its own root). The single unguarded call sat exactly where the directory is
most likely to be missing. Partial operations get used as if they were total in precisely
the code that runs when things have already gone wrong, and that code is the least tested.

**This is not a total-by-default replacement.** Totality hides a typo — `entriesOf("scr")`
returning `[]` is indistinguishable from an empty `src` — which is the right answer for a
diagnostic and the wrong one for a tool the model drives, where the model needs to be told
*why* it got nothing. So the caller picks, and the pick is visible at the call site: reach
for `entriesOf` to say "empty is fine here", and for the raw `listDir` behind a predicate to
say "this must exist, and I will explain it if it doesn't".

One honest limitation: a permission denial and a missing file give the same answer, because
`std.fs` offers no way to tell them apart. Asking for that is part of the upstream proposal.

[exists, isDir, isFile, listDir, readFile](std/fs.ssc)

```scalascript
/** The entries of `dir`, or `[]` when it is missing or is not a directory.
  *
  * For code that DESCRIBES a directory — a diagnostic, a survey, a "what is here" — where
  * "nothing" and "no such place" lead to the same next step. When they do not, use
  * `isDirSafe` and say which happened. */
def entriesOf(dir: String): List[String] =
  if isDirSafe(dir) then listDir(dir) else List()

/** The text of `file`, or `None` when it is missing or is not a regular file.
  *
  * `None` rather than `""`: an empty file is a real thing, and a caller that cannot tell it
  * from a missing one will eventually act on the difference. */
def textOf(file: String): Option[String] =
  if isFileSafe(file) then Some(readFile(file)) else None

/** The text of `file`, or `fallback`. For a caller that has a sensible default and does not
  * need to know which case it got. */
def textOr(file: String, fallback: String): String =
  textOf(file) match
    case Some(t) => t
    case None    => fallback

/** `isDir` that answers `false` instead of raising on a path that cannot be examined at all.
  *
  * A predicate that can raise is not a predicate: the caller ends up guarding the guard. */
def isDirSafe(path: String): Boolean =
  if !exists(path) then false else isDir(path)

/** `isFile` with the same promise. */
def isFileSafe(path: String): Boolean =
  if !exists(path) then false else isFile(path)
```
